part drawing that basically  depicts  the history of subway graffiti  it's pretty epic all these artists you  know were really  battling against being framed as  delinquents  as vandals for deciding to work on the  trains  the exhibition also places the graffiti  writers at the center of rap music's  origins  you're saying to yourself so this is  beatbox a track that  boscat produces in 1983 it effectively  becomes one of the most important tracks  in early hip-hop  history munsell attributes basquiat's  artistic break with his casting in the  movie  downtown 81. i've made my mark in the  world  and it's made its mark on me which gave  the young artist  access to canvas supplies and his own  studio  it's an absolute centerpiece of the show  pieces like hollywood africans would  help turn  basquiat into the figurehead of the  hip-hop artists  this painting is paying homage to hattie  mcdaniels  who was the first african-american to  win an academy award  for her role in gone with the wind he  really invented a  vocabulary all of his own through  choices  like gangsterism as well as heroism  he's talking about these dualities and  he's talking about the role  of the gangster that he's playing out in  the west coast art world  today basquiat is a conquering hero of  the art world  cemented in 2017 when this untitled work  sold for an astonishing 110 million  dollars  prompting one dealer to claim quote he's  now in the same league as francis bacon  and pablo picasso  basquiat would be 60 but died at age  27. he's buried in greenwood cemetery in  his native brooklyn  it's just a few miles down the road from  barclay center  where at various times this past season  the nba's brooklyn nets  sported uniforms and a court inspired by  basquiat yeah i mean he was a very  impactful artist  we had a chance to talk to the nets  kevin durant about it  a lot of guys said they want to try a  white jersey next year  the bosque jersey so we you know it's a  part of who we are  artists like that really are in our dna  as a team and as an  organization and as a city had you known  him of him before  yeah for sure knew his story tried to  look for some of his arts hard to get  but  it's good that we can represent him in  some way and have our own little art  piece on our jerseys as well  the nets also took the concept off the  court  partnering with the department of  education to develop a basquiat focused  art curriculum  for 1400 middle and high school students  in brooklyn  are you surprised at how your students  responded to this  i am art teacher tina stevens helped  11th grader ricky molina with his design  for this tote bag  cut it up we kind of stitched everything  and then a lot of pictures that i took  because i am a photographer  so like my name it's like rxky  it's all over the place everybody says  it has it changed  how you think of yourself as an artist  now yes the beginning of the school year  i was really going through like  a phase of depression because of school  and everything even though there's dark  spaces on this  there's still light here and there a lot  of people go through struggles  that think that they can come out of but  everybody can come out of that struggle  you guys met in person for the first  time today  yeah middle schooler vera montiel worked  remotely with her teacher  james hagerty this one is called  hollywood africans  this is an artist who has a lot of depth  here's a lot of symbols in his work  and i felt as if he really had something  to say and i wanted the students to  enjoy it  all of this is basically my identity  vera's work features her love of singing  her family and its ecuadorian heritage  and social justice would you have  put social justice in a piece of art  before learning about basquiat  definitely not  i did not see that as art but learning  about it  and how he was like he would always talk  about that through his art  that made me rethink that no this does  mean art this is our  i can put it in my work it makes art  even more powerful  yes artists like basquiat  and lee quinones didn't have access to  that kind of education in school  which once upon a time forced them and  their groundbreaking peers  underground most of us ran in the  shadows because we had to  but not anymore it's amazing to be in a  museum and see the work  just the way we wanted it to be  the exhibition in boston runs through  july 25th and the artwork of about 150  students will be on public display  at barclays center august 8th and 9th  that program was funded by the joe and  clara sci foundation part of the team  owners  social justice fund i have to say first  of all the artwork is amazing in boston  it's incredible to see in person  but those young children the kids the  teenagers yeah  to hear them just be ignited and  enjoying art and  seeing themselves and that they can be  artists in a different way you have to  find what inspires you  and this really spoke to me and  understanding that any expression of  yourself can be art exactly whatever it  is  yeah such an arc you did a great job  great job great job

